// Route S7 (Ch7, MAP_ROUTE_S07): the Pont de la Logique Brisee, a huge glass-and-steel walkway hung
// over a gulf of "data fog" (falling digits), concrete piers carrying platforms, from Manga-Square's
// Grand Torii (south) to Reverse-City's Secteur Eta gate (north). contenu-jeu/chapitres/
// 07-reverse-city.md (MAP_ROUTE_S07): logic arrows, the violet reversal slabs of the Passerelle des
// Paradoxes, the collapsed walkways, Team Cocket's maintenance barriers, islands of translucent blue
// crystal grass; the sky turns from pink (Manga-Square) to violet, then to negative near Reverse-City.
// Primary `Building` (no floor): every floor is here. The void (2x2) follows the sky: `vide_aube`
// (pink, S7-A), `vide` (indigo, S7-B and S7-C, also the border), `vide_violet` (S7-D), `vide_negatif`
// (the gate). Prompted: the steel and concrete decks, the piers. Drawn in derive.rs: the void and its
// recoloured variants, the arrows (`fleche_*`, MB_SLIDE_*, painted on `beton`), the reversal slab
// (`retournement`, MB_SLIDE_SOUTH on `verre`, the source's fallback), and, without a prompt because
// Codex's quota ran out during wave 3 (give them prompts to redraw), the glass `verre`, the crystal
// grass, the gate, the screens, the barrier, the pylon. The route has no connection (both ends are
// warps): no seam. `pile` (2x2) comes before the 1x1 floors: a 1x1 stamp of metatile 513 or 524 would
// equal an extract layout (studio map hygiene).
(
    name: "SilRouteS07",
    primary: "Building",
    style: "Minimalist 16-bit sprite in the Pokemon Ruby/Emerald overworld style (Game Boy Advance), a futuristic broken bridge of glass and steel over a digital abyss. Simple, iconic, low detail: big flat colour areas, 2-3 shades per material, a 1-pixel dark outline, no texture noise, no gradients, no glow halos, no tiny decorations, no anti-aliasing, no letters. Palette: cold steel greys, pale cyan glass, raw concrete, deep indigo night, with a few flat accents of electric cyan, violet and warning yellow.",
    features: [
        (
            name: "vide",
            cells: ["##", "##"],
        ),
        (
            name: "vide_aube",
            cells: ["##", "##"],
        ),
        (
